  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "surgical_drain_section_1",
      "section_title": "Surgical drain",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical drain is a tube or device inserted into a wound, body cavity or operative site to remove accumulated fluid, blood, pus, air or other unwanted material."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It helps prevent fluid accumulation, permits monitoring of postoperative drainage and, in selected circumstances, reduces the risk of complications associated with collections."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical drains are commonly used following abdominal surgery, breast surgery, orthopaedic procedures, thoracic surgery and the drainage of selected abscesses."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a surgical drain, used to evacuate fluid, blood, pus or air from a surgical site or body cavity. It may be an open or closed system and may function by gravity, capillary action or suction, depending on its design.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Identification and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The components depend on the type of drain presented. A closed suction drain typically contains the following:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "es.oncolink.org"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Drain tube",
                      "Provides a pathway for fluid to leave the wound or cavity."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fenestrations or side holes",
                      "Allow fluid to enter the tube along the portion positioned within the operative site."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Drain tip",
                      "Distal end of the drain; may be open-ended or perforated depending on design."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Connecting tubing",
                      "Carries fluid from the drain to the collection reservoir."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Collection reservoir",
                      "Collects and allows measurement of drainage."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suction bulb or reservoir",
                      "Generates negative pressure in a closed suction system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Connector",
                      "Joins compatible tubing and components."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Clamp, if present",
                      "Controls flow during specific procedures or according to the device instructions."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fixation suture or securement device",
                      "Helps prevent accidental displacement."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Graduated markings",
                      "Allow estimation of the collected fluid volume."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: A Penrose drain does not normally have a suction bulb or closed collection reservoir. Always label only the components present on the instrument shown."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Properties and characteristics",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Commonly silicone, latex rubber or another medical-grade polymer."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flexibility: Must conform to the operative site without kinking excessively."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Drainage openings: May include fenestrations to facilitate fluid entry."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diameter: Selected according to the anticipated fluid, viscosity and site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Length: Depends on the surgical field and the required route."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Collection method: Open drainage, gravity drainage or closed suction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterility: Supplied sterile or prepared according to the intended clinical use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Radiopacity: Some drains contain radiopaque material to facilitate identification on imaging."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fixation: Many drains require sutures or another securement method."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Classification of surgical drains",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical drains are classified according to their mechanism and whether the drainage system is open or closed."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_6",
              "group_title": "A. According to mechanism",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Passive drains"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Drainage occurs through gravity, capillary action or pressure gradients."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Example: Penrose drain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected superficial wounds, cavities or abscess drainage where an open system is appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Active drains"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Negative pressure or suction helps draw fluid into a collection reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examples: Jackson-Pratt (JP) drain and Redivac drain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected postoperative wounds where controlled closed drainage is indicated."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_7",
              "group_title": "B. According to system design",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open drain: Fluid exits into a dressing or external collection arrangement; contamination risk may be greater."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Closed drain: Fluid passes through sealed tubing into a collection reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Closed suction drain: A closed system that uses negative pressure to facilitate drainage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gravity drain: Uses gravity to direct fluid into an appropriate collection system."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_8",
              "group_title": "C. According to material or structure",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Soft rubber or silicone drains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flexible perforated tube drains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Corrugated drains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rigid or semi-rigid drains designed for specific cavities or procedures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Important types of surgical drains",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Penrose drain",
                    "Passive, open drain"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A soft, flexible tube that allows fluid to escape onto a dressing or into an appropriate external collection arrangement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Simple and inexpensive."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Does not require a suction reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can permit ascending contamination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires careful dressing and skin care."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Jackson-Pratt (JP) drain",
                    "Closed suction drain"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A flexible perforated tube connected to a compressible bulb reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The compressed bulb creates negative pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used following breast, abdominal and selected reconstructive procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows measurement of the drainage volume."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires maintenance of the suction system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Redivac drain",
                    "Closed suction drain"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses a vacuum reservoir to provide negative pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used in selected orthopaedic and general surgical procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Collects fluid in a closed reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires monitoring of output and suction function."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Corrugated drain",
                    "Usually passive"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A flexible drain with a corrugated structure that facilitates drainage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used in selected wounds or cavities."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Does not usually require a suction reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May be associated with external contamination and dressing requirements."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "E. Chest drain",
                    "Thoracic drainage system"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tube inserted into the pleural space and connected to an appropriate drainage system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used for pneumothorax, haemothorax and selected pleural effusions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May use an underwater seal and/or regulated suction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires specific monitoring for air leaks, fluid output and respiratory complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important distinction: A chest drain is a specialised drainage system. Its underwater seal and pleural pressure requirements differ from those of a routine wound drain."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Principles of drainage",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The drain's purpose is to provide a controlled route for unwanted material to leave a wound or body cavity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its mechanism may involve:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gravity: Fluid flows towards a lower collection point."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Capillary action: Fluid moves along narrow spaces or material."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pressure gradients: Fluid moves from a higher-pressure area towards a lower-pressure area."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Negative pressure: Suction assists the movement of fluid into the drain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A drain only works effectively when it is appropriately positioned, patent and connected to a functioning collection system."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Indications and uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical drains may be used to:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove blood or serous fluid accumulating after surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Drain pus from selected infected cavities or abscesses."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduce fluid accumulation in selected operative spaces."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Monitor postoperative bleeding or fluid production."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Drain the pleural space of air or fluid using a chest drain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Facilitate drainage following selected breast, abdominal, plastic or orthopaedic procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Manage selected postoperative collections under surgical or image-guided care."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_12",
              "group_title": "Examples by surgical specialty",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Specialty",
                    "Example"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Breast surgery",
                      "Selected mastectomy or reconstructive procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "General surgery",
                      "Selected abdominal operations or abscess drainage"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Selected joint or soft-tissue operations"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Thoracic surgery",
                      "Pleural drainage using a chest tube"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Plastic surgery",
                      "Selected flap or reconstructive procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurosurgery",
                      "Selected intracranial or spinal drainage systems designed for that purpose"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield point: Drains are not required after every operation. Their use should be based on the procedure, expected collection, clinical indication and evidence-based practice."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Facilitates removal of fluid, blood or pus."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows postoperative output to be monitored."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May help identify abnormal bleeding or changes in fluid production."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Closed systems reduce environmental contamination compared with open systems."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suction drains can help evacuate fluid from selected spaces."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some drains can be managed outside hospital when appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Specialised drains can manage air and fluid in body cavities."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May introduce infection."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can cause pain or discomfort."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May become blocked, kinked or displaced."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can injure adjacent tissue or vessels."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause skin irritation or pressure injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can prolong wound care and require additional monitoring."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A drain can create a route for organisms to enter the wound."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unnecessary drains may increase morbidity without improving outcomes."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Infection"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Organisms may enter through the drain tract or contaminated connections, particularly with prolonged use or poor drain care."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Bleeding"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insertion or movement of a drain may injure vessels. Fresh blood or a sudden increase in bloody output requires assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Blockage or kinking"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clots, thick fluid, debris or compression can prevent drainage and lead to fluid accumulation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "4. Displacement or accidental removal"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The drain may no longer reach the intended collection site, reducing effectiveness or injuring tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "5. Pain, tissue injury or pressure damage"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Drains may irritate adjacent tissue, cause discomfort or contribute to pressure-related injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_drain_15.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "6. Retained drain fragment"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rarely, a drain may break during removal, requiring assessment and sometimes further intervention."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few universal contraindications because drain selection depends on the clinical problem."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoiding unnecessary drain insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assessing bleeding risk and nearby anatomical structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selecting an appropriate drain type and diameter."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoiding inappropriate placement through contaminated or vulnerable structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensuring that the collection system is suitable for the material being drained."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Securing the drain and documenting its insertion site and depth when relevant."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A drain should not be inserted blindly into a body cavity when safe placement cannot be assured."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Postoperative drain care",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a frequent OSCE topic."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_18",
              "group_title": "A. Inspection",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The insertion site for redness, swelling, leakage and discharge."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The drain tubing for kinks, compression or visible blockage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The collection reservoir for correct connections and function."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The fluid's colour, consistency and odour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The total output and changes over time."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_19",
              "group_title": "B. Output monitoring",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Document the volume at the appropriate interval and describe the appearance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Appearance",
                    "Possible interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Bright red blood",
                      "May indicate fresh bleeding, especially if increasing"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Dark red or blood-stained fluid",
                      "May occur during early postoperative drainage"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Serous, pale-yellow fluid",
                      "May be expected in some postoperative settings"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cloudy or purulent fluid",
                      "May indicate infection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Green or bilious fluid",
                      "May indicate a bile leak or communication with the gastrointestinal tract, depending on the operative site"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sudden cessation of output",
                      "May indicate resolution, blockage, kinking or displacement"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpret the output in relation to the operation, timing and patient's clinical condition. Appearance alone is not diagnostic."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_20",
              "group_title": "C. Maintaining a closed suction drain",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For a JP or similar drain:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Keep the reservoir and connections intact."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain suction according to the manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Empty and measure the contents using the prescribed aseptic technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Re-establish suction when required by the device design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Record output and assess the insertion site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not routinely irrigate, strip or milk a drain unless this is specifically indicated and authorised by the relevant clinical protocol."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_21",
              "group_title": "D. Drain removal",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Removal depends on the operation, drain type, clinical findings and output trend. There is no single output threshold suitable for every drain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Removal should be performed by an appropriately trained clinician using the relevant protocol. If resistance, severe pain or suspected breakage occurs, stop and reassess rather than applying force."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Open versus closed drains",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_22",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Open drain",
                    "Closed drain"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Example",
                      "Penrose drain",
                      "JP or Redivac drain"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Collection",
                      "Dressing or external arrangement",
                      "Sealed reservoir"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mechanism",
                      "Usually passive",
                      "Passive or suction-assisted"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Contamination risk",
                      "Generally greater",
                      "Generally lower when maintained correctly"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Output measurement",
                      "Often less precise",
                      "Usually easier to measure"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical concern",
                      "Skin contamination and dressing care",
                      "Blockage, loss of suction or connection failure"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE questions and answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_23",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a surgical drain, used to remove unwanted fluid, blood, pus or air from an operative site or body cavity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Classify surgical drains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They may be classified as open or closed, passive or active, and by their construction and intended anatomical site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between a Penrose and a JP drain?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A Penrose is typically an open passive drain; a JP is a closed suction drain with a compressible bulb reservoir."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the function of the fenestrations?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They allow fluid to enter the drain through openings along the portion positioned within the operative site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What does sudden fresh blood in a drain suggest?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may indicate postoperative bleeding and requires prompt assessment, particularly if output is increasing or the patient is unstable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why might a drain stop producing fluid?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cavity may have stopped producing fluid, or the drain may be blocked, kinked, displaced or malfunctioning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Name four complications of surgical drains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Infection, bleeding, blockage, displacement, pain and tissue injury are important examples."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Are drains necessary after every operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. They should be used when clinically indicated because unnecessary drainage can cause complications without providing benefit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What is the purpose of a chest drain's underwater seal?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It allows air and fluid to leave the pleural space while helping prevent air from flowing back into it."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_drain_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_drain_group_24",
